      "writtenOn": "2026-10-12T14:19:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Requesting 32 SGEs increases the provider’s SQ allocation. With `tx-queue-size 4096`, mlx5 allocates 65,536 WQEBBs. If hardware accepts that size, `min_log2(dv_qp.sq.wqe_cnt)` is 16, which truncates to zero in the four-bit `dv_sq_log2sz` field. Debug TX then asserts, while release TX uses incorrect ring masks. The non-TSO QP path has the same issue.\n\nCould we validate the returned SQ size against the field and counter limits, and adjust the allocation or representation accordingly?",
      "revId": "9742d1d65ef3f709792c8e9a8abbdd2350605ae2",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2680e6b3_f5736b15",
        "filename": "src/plugins/rdma/device.c",
        "patchSetId": 68
      },
      "lineNbr": 977,
      "author": {
        "id": 333
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-12T19:59:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Addressed in PS69.\n\nThe initial DV WR reservation is now bounded using the power-of-two maximum WQE allocation. The returned SQ is inspected and retried with fewer WRs if still oversized; SQ/CQ counts are validated against the four-bit logarithms and 16-bit counters before storing them. The requested buffer/CQ capacities are preserved. On ConnectX-6, 4096-buffer QPs create successfully with SQ\u003d32768 and CQ\u003d8192, both with and without TSO. This device rejects an unbounded 4096-WR request before SQ inspection, which is why the initial bound is also needed.",
      "parentUuid": "024b53d4_ac9dc2ff",
      "revId": "9742d1d65ef3f709792c8e9a8abbdd2350605ae2",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "8025197e_7b6fb731",
        "filename": "src/plugins/rdma/output.c",
        "patchSetId": 68
      },
      "lineNbr": 1007,
      "author": {
        "id": 361
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-10-12T14:19:08Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "When the fast loop has built a regular-send prefix, `last` initially points at the next unused WQE. If the first GSO packet returns `NO_SLOTS`, we signal that unused WQE instead of the prefix’s last WQE, so the prefix has no completion.\n\nFor example, 255 regular packets followed by GSO on an empty 256-slot buffer ring can leave subsequent TSO traffic unable to progress. Could we pass the prefix’s actual last WQE into this helper?",
      "revId": "9742d1d65ef3f709792c8e9a8abbdd2350605ae2",
      "serverId": "6d2eb258-4fe2-443e-8a38-ca81da23d4c2"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "afbe0198_bc29f04f",
        "filename": "src/plugins/rdma/output.c",
        "patchSetId": 68
      },
      "lineNbr": 1007,
